{
  "id": "Compound::DB00491 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Miglitol, an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor, is linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through a chain of relationships that involve Acarbose, a closely related compound in the same class. The evidence suggests that Miglitol is included in the broader category of alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, which in turn includes Acarbose. Acarbose is known to treat Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, indicating a direct therapeutic relationship. The lowest common ancestor of Miglitol and Acarbose is the glucosidase inhibitor class, highlighting a shared biological mechanism. The integration of these pathways suggests that Miglitol may exert its effects through a mechanism that influences glucose metabolism, potentially contributing to the management of diabetes. The evidence supports a causal link between Miglitol and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, mediated through its inclusion in the alpha-glucosidase inhibitor class and its therapeutic relationship with Acarbose.",
